<h2>Scene by scene</h2>
<ol class="cl-steps">
  <li><b>The damaged pipe</b>
  The video opens inside an old sewer: cracks, offset joints, roots. This is what a camera inspection finds in a clay or cast iron lateral that has been in the ground for fifty or more years. On a real job the camera recording is made within a day of lining, and every defect and branch connection is marked by footage so the crew knows what it is lining through. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/repair">cured in place pipe repair</a> page explains which of those defects a liner can fix and which need excavation first.</li>

  <li><b>Cleaning</b>
  The pipe is cleared before anything else. In the animation the debris simply disappears; in the field this is an hour or more of hydro-jetting, root cutting and, for cast iron, descaling, followed by a second camera pass to confirm the wall is clean and to measure the exact length. A liner over scale or grease is a liner that fails early, which is why this is the step good crews refuse to rush.</li>

  <li><b>The liner goes in</b>
  A flexible tube saturated with resin enters the pipe through an existing opening and travels to the far end. The video shows it as a smooth sleeve; in practice it is either inverted, turned inside out down the pipe by air or water pressure so the resin side meets the wall, or pulled into place by a winch and then inflated by a bladder. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/installation">cured in place pipe installation</a> page covers both methods and when each is used.</li>

  <li><b>Pressed against the wall</b>
  Pressure inside the tube pushes it outward until it conforms to the pipe, filling cracks and bridging gaps at the joints. This pressure is held for the entire cure. The liner takes the shape of whatever is there, which is why a sag in the old pipe becomes a sag in the new one, and why bellies are repaired by digging rather than lining.</li>

  <li><b>Curing</b>
  The resin hardens. The animation shows a color change; the real process is hot water or steam circulated through the liner for one to three hours while sensors track temperature, or a train of ultraviolet lamps pulled through a glass-reinforced liner. After the heat, a controlled cool-down keeps the liner pressurized until it is below about 100 degrees F so it does not shrink away from the wall. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/equipment">cured in place pipe equipment</a> page describes the boilers, steam units and UV trains that do this.</li>

  <li><b>Branch connections reopened</b>
  If the pipe has side connections, the liner has covered them. A camera-guided robotic cutter is driven in and grinds each one open to a clean, round hole. The animation may skip this; a real job cannot.</li>

  <li><b>The finished pipe</b>
  The closing shot is a smooth, jointless pipe inside the old one, with the yard above untouched. On a real job this is the final camera recording you should receive, along with the pre-lining recording and the cure log. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/liner-testing">cured in place pipe liner testing</a> page explains what else is checked on larger projects.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What the animation cannot show you</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Time.</strong> A residential lining job is a full working day; the video compresses it into seconds.</li>
  <li><strong>Judgment.</strong> Deciding whether the pipe should be lined at all, where to access it, how thick the liner needs to be and how long to cure it is the skill you are paying for. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/training-and-certifications">training and certifications</a> page lists what to look for.</li>
  <li><strong>Money.</strong> The whole point of the method, for most homeowners, is what it costs compared with the trench. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/cost">cured in place pipe cost</a> page has the real figures from completed jobs.</li>
</ul>

